// =============================================================================
//  GRADELUX FILMLAB  —  Version 9.0 (18 film stocks + 16 Cine16 secondary profiles)
//  Film stock & print emulation engine for DaVinci Resolve Studio (DCTL)
//  (c) Gradelux. All rights reserved.
// =============================================================================
//
//  COLOUR SPACE CONTRACT
//  ---------------------
//    Input  : DaVinci Wide Gamut / DaVinci Intermediate
//    Output : DaVinci Wide Gamut / DaVinci Intermediate
//
//    No colour space conversion is performed and there is no input selector.
//    Place FilmLab after the input CST (or inside an RCM timeline set to
//    DWG/Intermediate) and before the output CST / DRT.
//
//  VERSION 6
//  ---------
//    Production foundation built on the Version 5 diagnostic build. Its
//    three stocks (Verona 250D, Verdant 250D, Midnight 800T) are kept exactly
//    as they were; five stocks are added (Aurelia 400, Solara 200, Moss 250D,
//    Chroma 100, Silvergrain 100). A developer-only Stage View selector sits
//    at the end of the controls; at its default (Normal) it has no effect.
//
//  VERSION 6.1
//  -----------
//    Refines two stocks only: Moss 250D (more contrast, deeper blacks, richer
//    density) and Chroma 100 (much less saturation, colour carried by dye
//    density instead). Only their stock definitions changed; no shared
//    function changed, and the other six stocks, the prints and all controls
//    produce bit-identical output to Version 6 (tests/math_test_v6_1.c).
//
//  VERSION 7
//  ---------
//    Adds five stocks, appended after Silvergrain 100 so existing dropdown
//    positions are unchanged: Tungsten 500T, Halcyon 400, Argent 200,
//    Nocturne 800, Pastelle 400. The eight Version 6.1 stocks, the prints,
//    all controls and every shared function are unchanged; only new stock
//    definitions were added (verified bit-for-bit, tests/math_test_v7.c).
//
//  VERSION 8
//  ---------
//    Adds an independent SECONDARY FILM LOOK engine: the sixteen Gradelux
//    Cine16 film colour profiles, integrated from the original Cine16 DCTL
//    (1B . Film Color Profile v2.2) with their colour science unchanged (see
//    CINE16 SECONDARY ENGINE). Secondary Film Look = Off is a true bypass:
//    FilmLab then runs exactly the Version 7 code path and produces
//    bit-identical output (tests/math_test_v8.c).
//
//  VERSION 9
//  ---------
//    - Middle-grey anchoring: every primary stock maps 18% grey to 18% grey
//      at neutral settings. A stock's former print-down density is now a
//      black density (full in the deep shadows, zero at grey and above), and
//      a final neutral gain removes the remaining second-order grey shift.
//      Contrast, toe, shoulder and colour are unchanged; prints and the Cine16
//      profiles are unchanged (their grey behaviour is documented).
//    - Argent and Nocturne redesigned; five stocks added (Patina, Opaline,
//      Glacier, Terracotta, Velour); stock names without numbers (internal
//      IDs and dropdown positions unchanged).
//    - New controls: Exposure, Temperature, Tint (before the film engines)
//      and Split Toning (after grading). All bypass exactly at their defaults.
//
//  PROCESSING ORDER
//  ----------------
//    1. Decode DaVinci Intermediate -> linear DWG -> linear working space
//       (Rec.2020 primaries, internal only; see COLORIMETRY).
//    1b. PRIMARY CORRECTIONS: Exposure (scene-linear gain), then Temperature
//       and Tint (chromatic adaptation).
//    2. FILM LOOK ("negative" stage): the selected creative look, scaled by
//       Creative Intensity.
//    2b. SECONDARY FILM LOOK (Cine16): when not Off, the result so far is
//       encoded to DaVinci Intermediate, processed by the original Cine16
//       engine (DWG / Intermediate in and out) and decoded again. With the
//       primary look Off the input goes to Cine16 directly, unconverted.
//    3. FILM PRINT: the selected print emulation, scaled by Print Strength
//       and shaped by Print Contrast / Density / Saturation / Highlight
//       Rolloff. It builds on whatever the look produced.
//    4. FILM ADJUSTMENTS: Film Color Density, Film Color Separation,
//       Film Highlight Warmth, Film Shadow Tone.
//    5. VERSION 1 GRADING CONTROLS: Film Density, Subtractive Saturation,
//       Film Contrast, Shadow Depth, Highlight Roll-Off.
//    5b. SPLIT TONING: shadow / midtone / highlight tints.
//    6. Linear working -> linear DWG -> DaVinci Intermediate.
//    7. Global Intensity: final mix with the untouched input, done in
//       DaVinci Intermediate so the fade is perceptually even (this is why
//       it follows the encode).
//
//    Stages 2 and 3 depend only on the pixel's RGB and their own settings:
//    no image coordinates, neighbours, time or randomness. Each is a
//    deterministic RGB -> RGB function, so "look only", "print only" and
//    "look + print" can each be sampled exactly into a 3D LUT. With every
//    other control at default, the DCTL output IS that configuration.
//
//  SIGNED / EXTENDED VALUES
//  ------------------------
//    No 0–1 clamp is applied to image data. Negatives (out-of-gamut colours,
//    sensor noise) and highlights far above 1.0 linear pass through every
//    stage. All gains are finite and positive; tone curves use |x| so
//    negative values get a finite, sign-preserving response.
//
//  IDENTITY
//  --------
//    Film Look = Off and Film Print = None with every control at default (or
//    Global Intensity at 0%) returns the input bit-for-bit (processing is
//    skipped). Creative Intensity 0% bypasses the look exactly; Print
//    Strength 0% bypasses the print exactly.
//
//  ARCHITECTURE
//  ------------
//    GlxProfile  : constants of the Version 1 operators.
//    GlxLook     : the characteristic numbers of a look OR a print. Looks
//                  and prints share the operators (colour response, dye
//                  coupling, characteristic curves) but run them in their
//                  own order with their own settings (see FILM LOOK ENGINE
//                  and FILM PRINT ENGINE).
//    GlxFilmAdj  : the four film adjustment sliders, normalised.
//    GlxControls : the Version 1 sliders, normalised.
// =============================================================================


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  USER INTERFACE  (pipeline order: corrections -> look -> secondary look -> print -> film adjustments
//  -> grading -> split toning -> output -> Global Intensity)
//  Sliders use a -100..100 / 0..100 scale; all defaults are neutral.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
